  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "I built the dashboard and the alert rule. The rule is enabled and has already run once with status `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** About 14,000 web requests with timestamps from Oct 4 to Dec 3, 2026, so much of the sample data is dated in the future. Each request records:\n- the response code: 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441);\n- the bytes sent, the client IP and the source and destination countries;\n- the URL, file extension, browser, machine OS and tags.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-9a515b69 Web logs overview\"** has 7 panels: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-9a515b69-web-logs-overview-2562ef2d027d\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct client IPs)\n- Requests over time, split by response code\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Bytes transferred over time\n- Top 10 source countries\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and average bytes\n\nThe default time range runs from 7 days ago to 60 days ahead, because so much of the data is future-dated.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-9a515b69 5xx spike\"** (id `03055a25-8a28-4d73-97b3-df1541a84275`) runs every minute. It fires when more than 10 requests with a 5xx response code (500\u2013599) arrive in a 5-minute window, using the `timestamp` field. I checked the filter directly against the index and it matches all 441 of the 503 responses.\n\nThe rule has no action or connector attached, so when it fires the alert only shows up in Kibana and no notification is sent.",
